tests/ui/check/warn_cost_unknown.sky:2:5: note: cost of `until`: unbounded (loop at line 4)
  |
2 | def until(v: Vector[N]) -> Vector[N]:
  |     ^^^^^
  = note: the static cost model has no bound for it

tests/ui/check/warn_cost_unknown.sky:3:5: warning: cannot prove that `until` meets `cost flops <= 100 * N`
  |
3 |     cost flops <= 100 * N
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this contract is not proven
4 |     while v[0] > 1:
  |     --------------- this has no known bound on its cost
  = note: the cost has no upper bound: loop at line 4
  = note: the model bounds a counted loop (`for i in range(10)`, `range(len(v))`); a loop with another condition, a `break`, or a recursion has no bound

tests/ui/check/warn_cost_unknown.sky:5:13: note: [PAR-001] this elementwise `mul` runs on 8 threads once it has at least 262144 elements
  |
5 |         v = v * 2
  |             ^^^^^
  = note: because its size is known only when the program runs; below the threshold splitting costs more than it saves
  = note: because every element is independent, so the result is bit-identical to the sequential one whatever the split
  = note: because its only failure is an overflow (or a zero divisor) of the whole operation, found after the elements are computed and reported the same way whichever thread found it
  = note: because native code runs it on 8 threads too from 262144 elements (a compiled kernel computes an element so fast that splitting pays only from there), in the same chunks, and resolves a failure by the same rule, so its result and error are the same

tests/ui/check/warn_cost_unknown.sky:5:13: note: [MEM-001] `v * 2` reuses `v`'s buffer: no allocation in this loop
  |
5 |         v = v * 2
  |             ^^^^^
  = note: `v` is not used again after this on any path, and has the shape of the result
  = note: applied by the `lifetimes` analysis of the SIR interpreter

tests/ui/check/warn_cost_unknown.sky:8:5: note: cost of `forever`: unbounded (recursive call to `forever` at line 10)
  |
